  "blocks": {
    "question-body": {
      "id": "question-body",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "question-ask",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A circle has no corners, yet chain a few turning circles end to end and a pen on the last one draws a musical note with sharp corners. The outline's Fourier coefficients give each circle its radius, speed and starting angle. As circles are added from the largest down, the pen's curve comes ever closer to the outline, leaving only ripples at the corners that never vanish completely."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "circles-body": {
      "id": "circles-body",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "circles-explain",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "One circle can only draw a circle. Put a circle of radius 0.36 on its rim, turning twice the other way while the large one turns once, and the pen's curve gains three rounded corners. The curve is:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "two-circles-equation",
          "type": "math",
          "value": "z(t)=e^{\\,it}+0.36\\,e^{-2it}"
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "circles-scene": {
      "id": "circles-scene",
      "type": "component",
      "definitionId": "circle-chain",
      "parameters": {}
    },
    "fit-body": {
      "id": "fit-body",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "fit-explain",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The discrete Fourier transform gives each circle's radius and starting angle. Take M = 1024 evenly spaced points zₖ along the outline of the note, then compute each coefficient cₙ with the formula below. The figure keeps only the N largest circles, chained from the largest down. The dashed line is the target outline; the error on the right is the root mean square distance between each point and the pen, relative to the size of the outline."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "dft-equation",
          "type": "math",
          "value": "c_n=\\frac1M\\sum_{k=0}^{M-1} z_k\\,e^{-2\\pi i nk/M},\\qquad M=1024"
        },
        {
          "id": "fit-corners",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The error falls quickly with the number of circles: about 64% with 1 circle, about 8% with 8, about 1% with 32 and under 0.5% with 64. Corners are the hardest part: with few circles the curve overshoots each corner and swings back, leaving ripples. With more circles the ripples hug the corner more closely, but they never vanish completely."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "fit-scene": {
      "id": "fit-scene",
      "type": "component",
      "definitionId": "fourier-fit",
      "parameters": {
        "shape": "note",
        "terms": [
          1,
          2,
          4,
          8,
          16,
          32,
          64
        ]
      }
    },
    "try-body": {
      "id": "try-body",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "try-predict",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A star has ten sharp corners and ten straight edges. With only two circles the curve already has five corners; four circles sharpen the points and straighten the edges; at sixteen the edges are straight and only the corners are slightly round; at sixty-four it is almost indistinguishable from the star. Two circles give five corners because of symmetry: a star looks the same after a fifth of a turn, so only circles whose n leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 5 are non-zero."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "try-scene": {
      "id": "try-scene",
      "type": "component",
      "definitionId": "fourier-star",
      "parameters": {
        "shape": "star",
        "terms": [
          2,
          4,
          16,
          64
        ]
      }
    },
    "code-body": {
      "id": "code-body",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "code-explain",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This is the function series that computes the lesson's coefficients: it takes a loop of evenly spaced points on an outline and returns the first circle's centre c₀ and the other circles, largest radius first. Give it other points and it draws another outline."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "series-code": {
      "id": "series-code",
      "type": "code",
      "language": "typescript",
      "title": "Computing the Fourier coefficients",
      "origin": {
        "anchor": "symbol:learn/scenes/epicycles.ts#series"
      },
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "next-body": {
      "id": "next-body",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "next-try",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Any closed outline can be broken into a chain of turning circles this way: the Fourier coefficients give each circle its size, speed and starting angle, and the more circles, the closer the curve comes to the outline, while the ripples at the corners only get shorter. Change the shape in epicycles.ts, predict from its symmetry how many circles it needs, then check."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "back-to-site",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "link",
              "href": "../../../promo/lecture.json",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Watch the 60-second film made with Presmium."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "question-equation": {
      "id": "question-equation",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "series-equation",
          "type": "math",
          "value": "z(t)=c_0+\\sum_{n\\ne 0} c_n e^{\\,int},\\qquad 0\\le t<2\\pi"
        }
      ]
    },
    "question-terms": {
      "id": "question-terms",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "question-reading",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The formula reads each point of the plane as a complex number z = x + iy. Each term cₙeⁱⁿᵗ is a circle: its radius is |cₙ|, it turns n times while the pen goes round once, and the sign of n sets the direction; c₀ is the centre of the first circle."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "web"
      ]
    },
    "question-keywords": {
      "id": "question-keywords",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "question-keywords--draw",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Turning circles → a note with corners"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "question-keywords--coefficients",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Radius, speed, starting angle: Fourier coefficients"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "slides",
        "lecture"
      ]
    },
    "try-keywords": {
      "id": "try-keywords",
      "type": "text",
      "content": [
        {
          "id": "try-keywords--claim",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "More circles, a closer shape; ripples stay at the corners"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "only": [
        "slides",
        "lecture"
      ]
    }
  },
